librecomp: say what the cartridge was asked for

A transfer here costs nothing -- the bytes are in a host buffer and the copy is
a memcpy -- where on the console it costs the time the parallel interface takes
to clock them out. Whether that is where a game's loading pauses go is a
question about the game rather than about the runtime, so the runtime can answer
it: N64B_PI_LOG reports every transfer with its address, its size and a running
total against the wall clock.

It was worth the twenty lines. Banjo-Tooie's loading pauses last two to three
seconds on a console and the bursts of cartridge traffic behind them are 0.6 to
0.9 MB, which is a sixth of a second at the 5.4 MB/s a cartridge manages -- ten
megabytes over the whole of its first two and a half minutes. Modelling the
parallel interface's own timing would have been a day's work for five per cent
of the difference, and the pauses are the processor decompressing a world.

